  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=(x-1)^{3}?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[1,\\infty )$\n- $\u2200\\,y\\in R,$ $\u2203\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is **onto**",
  "worked_solution": "First, we graph the function. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14101062/assets/80630fae7c4f1e29d855e9a68917156ccf3ecbefd0efc7bda5a9bd0e903c90db.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $\u2200\\,y\\in Y,$ $\u2203\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph covers all $y$-values, so the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$, then for all $y$ contained within $R,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is a surjection of $R$ onto $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
